  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a blender work?",
      "a": "A motor spins sharp, angled blades very fast. They chop the food and throw it outwards, and the swirling flow keeps bringing more food back to the blades until it is smooth.",
      "asks": [
        "What spins in a blender?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does food swirl down into the blades?",
      "a": "The blades fling food up the sides of the jug, and it falls back down the middle. This vortex keeps carrying food down into the blades again and again.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a blender vortex?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do blender jugs have ridges inside?",
      "a": "Without them the whole mixture might just spin round with the blades. The ridges break up the spinning so food tumbles back into the blades.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is a blender jug not round inside?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why should you add liquid to a blender?",
      "a": "Liquid lets the food move and flow, so the vortex can form. Without it, food can jam above the blades in an air pocket and not blend.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my blender just spin without blending?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why must the lid be on?",
      "a": "The vortex throws food upwards, and hot liquids can produce steam pressure. The lid keeps everything in and stops splashes and burns.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does hot soup explode in a blender?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Blender blades often spin at 20,000 revolutions per minute or more.",
    "The swirling vortex in a blender repeatedly draws food back down into the blades."
  ],
